WEBVTT

00:00.584 --> 00:02.000
What I want to do
in this video is

00:02.000 --> 00:04.140
to see whether the
power rule is giving us

00:04.140 --> 00:06.055
results that at least
seem reasonable.

00:06.055 --> 00:08.480
This is by no means a
proof of the power rule,

00:08.480 --> 00:12.720
but at least we'll feel a little
bit more comfortable using it.

00:12.720 --> 00:18.190
So let's say that f
of x is equal to x.

00:18.190 --> 00:20.440
The power rule tells
us that f prime

00:20.440 --> 00:23.270
of x is going to
be equal to what?

00:23.270 --> 00:25.990
Well, x is the same thing
as x to the first power.

00:25.990 --> 00:28.194
So n is implicitly
1 right over here.

00:28.194 --> 00:29.360
So we bring the 1 out front.

00:29.360 --> 00:32.740
It'll be 1 times x to
the 1 minus 1 power.

00:32.740 --> 00:36.200
So it's going to be 1 times
x to the 0 power. x to the 0

00:36.200 --> 00:36.730
is just 1.

00:36.730 --> 00:38.920
So it's just going
to be equal to 1.

00:38.920 --> 00:40.560
Now, does that makes
conceptual sense

00:40.560 --> 00:43.100
if we actually try to
visualize these functions?

00:43.100 --> 00:47.370
So let me actually try
to graph these functions.

00:47.370 --> 00:50.700
So that's my y-axis.

00:50.700 --> 00:52.230
This is my x-axis.

00:52.230 --> 00:55.050
And let me graph y equals x.

00:55.050 --> 00:57.320
So y is equal to f of x here.

00:57.320 --> 00:58.960
So y is equal to x.

00:58.960 --> 01:01.180
So it looks something like that.

01:01.180 --> 01:04.209
So y is equal to x.

01:04.209 --> 01:06.000
Or this is f of x
is equal to x, or y

01:06.000 --> 01:08.560
is equal to this f of
x right over there.

01:08.560 --> 01:12.540
Now, actually, let me
just call that f of x just

01:12.540 --> 01:13.910
to not confuse you.

01:13.910 --> 01:15.930
So this right over
here is f of x

01:15.930 --> 01:17.870
is equal to x that I
graphed right over here.

01:17.870 --> 01:21.330
y is equal to f of x,
which is equal to x.

01:21.330 --> 01:23.280
And now, let me
graph the derivative.

01:23.280 --> 01:24.850
Let me graph f prime of x.

01:24.850 --> 01:26.370
That's saying it's 1.

01:26.370 --> 01:27.850
That's saying it's 1 for all x.

01:27.850 --> 01:30.821
Regardless of what x is,
it's going to be equal to 1.

01:30.821 --> 01:32.320
Is this consistent
with what we know

01:32.320 --> 01:35.380
about derivatives and
slopes and all the rest?

01:35.380 --> 01:36.890
Well, let's look
at our function.

01:36.890 --> 01:41.560
What is the slope of the tangent
line right at this point?

01:41.560 --> 01:45.130
Well, right over here, this
has slope 1 continuously.

01:45.130 --> 01:47.060
Or it has a constant slope of 1.

01:47.060 --> 01:49.220
Slope is equal to 1
no matter what x is.

01:49.220 --> 01:50.212
It's a line.

01:50.212 --> 01:51.795
And for a line, the
slope is constant.

01:51.795 --> 01:54.840
So over here, the
slope is indeed 1.

01:54.840 --> 01:57.390
If you go to this point over
here, the slope is indeed 1.

01:57.390 --> 02:00.890
If you go over here,
the slope is indeed 1.

02:00.890 --> 02:05.260
So we've got a pretty
valid response there.

02:05.260 --> 02:08.100
Now, let's try something
where the slope might change.

02:08.100 --> 02:13.740
So let's say I have g of
x is equal to x squared.

02:13.740 --> 02:16.610
The power rule tells
us that g prime of x

02:16.610 --> 02:18.550
would be equal to what?

02:18.550 --> 02:19.700
Well, n is equal to 2.

02:19.700 --> 02:23.080
So it's going to be 2
times x to the 2 minus 1.

02:23.080 --> 02:25.660
Or it's going to be equal
to 2 x to the first power.

02:25.660 --> 02:27.860
It's going to be equal to 2x.

02:27.860 --> 02:31.067
So let's see if this
makes a reasonable sense.

02:31.067 --> 02:33.400
And I'm going to try to graph
this one a little bit more

02:33.400 --> 02:33.899
precisely.

02:38.470 --> 02:42.360
Let's see how precisely
I can graph it.

02:42.360 --> 02:44.717
So this is the x-axis, y-axis.

02:44.717 --> 02:46.050
Let me mark some stuff off here.

02:46.050 --> 02:50.990
So this is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5.

02:50.990 --> 02:55.322
This is 1, 2, 3, 4.

02:55.322 --> 02:58.550
1, 2, 3, 4.

02:58.550 --> 03:01.210
So g of x.

03:01.210 --> 03:02.930
When x is 0, it's 0.

03:02.930 --> 03:05.390
When x is 1, it is 1.

03:05.390 --> 03:06.780
When x is negative 1, it's 1.

03:06.780 --> 03:09.350
When x is 2, it is 4.

03:09.350 --> 03:11.849
So that puts us right
over there-- 1, 2, 3, 4.

03:11.849 --> 03:12.890
Puts us right over there.

03:12.890 --> 03:15.170
When x is negative
2, you get to 4.

03:15.170 --> 03:15.917
It's a parabola.

03:15.917 --> 03:17.250
You've seen this for many years.

03:20.575 --> 03:22.200
I put that point a
little bit too high.

03:22.200 --> 03:23.660
It looks something like this.

03:26.270 --> 03:29.020
Actually, the last two points I
graphed are a little bit weird.

03:29.020 --> 03:31.840
So this might be
right over here.

03:31.840 --> 03:34.690
So it looks something like this.

03:34.690 --> 03:35.980
It looks something like that.

03:35.980 --> 03:38.550
And then, when you
come over here,

03:38.550 --> 03:41.810
it looks something like that.

03:41.810 --> 03:42.490
It's symmetric.

03:42.490 --> 03:45.250
So I'm trying my best
to draw it reasonably.

03:45.250 --> 03:46.130
So there you go.

03:46.130 --> 03:51.860
That's the graph of g of x.
g of x is equal to x squared.

03:51.860 --> 03:54.360
Now, let's graph g prime of
x or what the power rule is

03:54.360 --> 03:56.950
telling us a g prime of x is.

03:56.950 --> 03:59.240
So g prime of x is equal to 2x.

03:59.240 --> 04:03.230
So that's just a line that goes
through the origin of slope 2.

04:03.230 --> 04:04.920
So it looks something like that.

04:04.920 --> 04:07.400
When x is equal to
1, y is equal to 2.

04:07.400 --> 04:11.670
When x is equal to 2, y
or g of x is equal to 4.

04:11.670 --> 04:16.540
So it looks something like this.

04:16.540 --> 04:18.720
Let me try my best to
draw a straight line.

04:18.720 --> 04:20.839
It looks something like this.

04:20.839 --> 04:25.010
Now, does this make sense?

04:25.010 --> 04:26.800
Well, if you just
eyeball it really fast,

04:26.800 --> 04:28.670
if you look at this
point right over here--

04:28.670 --> 04:33.190
and you want to think about
the slope of the tangent line.

04:33.190 --> 04:36.470
Let me do this in a color that
pops out a little bit more.

04:36.470 --> 04:39.490
So the tangent line would
look something like this.

04:39.490 --> 04:43.650
So it looks like it has a
reasonably high negative slope.

04:43.650 --> 04:44.150
Yeah.

04:44.150 --> 04:45.190
It's definitely
a negative slope,

04:45.190 --> 04:46.814
and it's a pretty
steep negative slope.

04:52.590 --> 04:58.460
For x is equal to negative
2, g prime of negative 2

04:58.460 --> 05:04.140
is equal to 2 times negative 2,
which is equal to negative 4.

05:04.140 --> 05:06.621
So this is claiming that
the slope at this point-- so

05:06.621 --> 05:08.120
this right over
here is negative 4--

05:08.120 --> 05:09.703
is saying that the
slope of this point

05:09.703 --> 05:12.220
is negative 4. m is
equal to negative 4.

05:12.220 --> 05:13.740
That looks about right.

05:13.740 --> 05:16.830
It's a fairly steep
negative slope.

05:16.830 --> 05:19.370
Now, what happens if
you go right over here

05:19.370 --> 05:21.410
when x is equal to 0?

05:21.410 --> 05:24.090
Well, our derivative--
if you say g prime of 0--

05:24.090 --> 05:27.730
is telling us that the slope of
our original function, g, at x

05:27.730 --> 05:31.710
is equal to 0 is 2 times 0 is 0.

05:31.710 --> 05:32.874
Well, does that make sense?

05:32.874 --> 05:34.540
Well, if we go to our
original parabola,

05:34.540 --> 05:36.289
it does indeed make sense.

05:36.289 --> 05:37.830
That's the slope of
the tangent line.

05:37.830 --> 05:39.720
The tangent line looks
something like this.

05:39.720 --> 05:40.520
We're at a minimum point.

05:40.520 --> 05:41.353
We're at the vertex.

05:41.353 --> 05:44.430
The slope really
does look to be 0.

05:44.430 --> 05:47.340
And what if you
go right over here

05:47.340 --> 05:51.120
to x equals 2, the slope
of the tangent line?

05:51.120 --> 05:54.780
Well, over here, the tangent
line looks something like this.

05:54.780 --> 05:59.040
It looks like a fairly
steep positive slope.

05:59.040 --> 06:02.672
What is our derivative telling
us based on the power rule?

06:02.672 --> 06:04.130
So this is essentially
saying, hey,

06:04.130 --> 06:06.160
tell me what the slope
of the tangent line for g

06:06.160 --> 06:09.630
is when x is equal to 2.

06:09.630 --> 06:10.630
Well, we figured it out.

06:10.630 --> 06:11.870
It's going to be 2 times x.

06:11.870 --> 06:14.170
It's going to be 2 times
2, which is equal to 4.

06:14.170 --> 06:17.580
It's telling us that the
slope over here is 4.

06:17.580 --> 06:19.800
And I'm just using m.
m is often the letter

06:19.800 --> 06:21.089
used to denote slope.

06:21.089 --> 06:23.130
They're saying that the
slope of the tangent line

06:23.130 --> 06:26.430
there is 4, which seems
completely, completely

06:26.430 --> 06:27.310
reasonable.

06:27.310 --> 06:29.260
So I encourage you to
try this out yourself.

06:29.260 --> 06:32.700
I encourage you to try
to estimate the slopes

06:32.700 --> 06:34.729
by calculating, by
taking points closer

06:34.729 --> 06:36.020
and closer around those points.

06:36.020 --> 06:37.980
And you'll see that
the power rule really

06:37.980 --> 06:41.860
does give you results
that actually make sense.
